{"created_at":"2026-07-22T06:00:00-07:00","fingerprint":"6add6692e185312d","followup_required":false,"observation_id":"evd_obs_ngram_capacity_vram_linear","possible_confounds":["allocator caching; early readings understate steady-state"],"run_ids":["run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_ngram_mult_256_s43_6f1d19458417ae46","run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_ngram_mult_256_s44_f4f0692d10043b71","run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_ngram_mult_256_s45_675cff61a14cbff2","run_exp_ngram_capacity_1_ngram_mult_128_s43_1123e0dc759bd422","run_exp_ngram_capacity_1_ngram_mult_128_s44_88b98359525f1ac1","run_exp_ngram_capacity_1_ngram_mult_128_s45_7b90bc8e8ad86e19"],"statement":"Steady-state VRAM scales ~linearly with NGRAM_TABLE_MULT: 64x~14GB, 128x~57GB, 256x~113GB (full RMSProp state on the sparse tables). Implies 512x ~226GB -> OOM on a 143GB H200; 256x is the ceiling of the feasible sweep.","status":"exploratory","tags":["ngram","capacity","vram"]}
{"created_at":"2026-07-22T06:00:00-07:00","fingerprint":"a740553b0f6575a7","followup_required":false,"observation_id":"evd_obs_ngram_dense_params_invariant","possible_confounds":["num_params_M may exclude sparse embeddings; VRAM still differs"],"run_ids":["run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_ngram_mult_256_s43_6f1d19458417ae46","run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_ngram_mult_256_s44_f4f0692d10043b71","run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_ngram_mult_256_s45_675cff61a14cbff2"],"statement":"Dense num_params_M is identical (94.4) across NGRAM_TABLE_MULT 64/128/256: capacity lives entirely in the sparse VE hash tables (O(1) gather). Confirms the comparison is compute-matched, not a params-for-quality trade.","status":"exploratory","tags":["ngram","capacity","compute_fair"]}
{"created_at":"2026-07-22T06:00:00-07:00","fingerprint":"4254247105cccaff","followup_required":false,"observation_id":"evd_obs_256x_reproduced_three_windows","possible_confounds":["same box/corpus; cross-hardware untested"],"run_ids":["run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_ngram_mult_256_s43_6f1d19458417ae46","run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_ngram_mult_256_s44_f4f0692d10043b71","run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_ngram_mult_256_s45_675cff61a14cbff2"],"statement":"The 256x effect reproduced across three independent windows: seed-42 pilot (-0.00363), the 43/44/45 discovery (-0.00405), and the cycle-3 control-vs-treat contrast (-0.00405). Low temporal drift; robust.","status":"replicated","tags":["ngram","capacity","robustness"]}
{"created_at":"2026-07-22T06:00:00-07:00","fingerprint":"306434aa59e3aef0","followup_required":true,"observation_id":"evd_obs_per_step_gap_too_noisy","possible_confounds":["single-batch val noise"],"run_ids":["run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_ngram_mult_256_s43_6f1d19458417ae46"],"statement":"Logged per-step train_val_gap swings +/-0.1 (single-batch val), unusable as an overfitting signal at a step. A memorization check needs smoothed final train-loss (observable_curves.json); hence the 256x memorization question is deferred, not answered.","status":"exploratory","tags":["observable","gap","memorization"]}
{"created_at":"2026-07-22T10:45:00-07:00","fingerprint":"802775a88b169956","followup_required":false,"observation_id":"evd_obs_ngram_256x_transfers_2766","possible_confounds":["single budget point (2766); one off-budget batch","diagnostic bypasses the adopt-gate by design"],"run_ids":["run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_baseline_s42_ca240859436174d3","run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_ngram_mult_256_s42_3b974ab61bdde5d3","run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_baseline_s43_c7b48f30c4c6eda7","run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_baseline_s44_4a331569b89bb007","run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_ngram_mult_256_s43_da12f6f24d398a6c","run_exp_ngram_capacity_2_ngram_mult_256_s44_961c3018d80acc62"],"statement":"DIAGNOSTIC (off-budget, 2766 steps, NOT an adopt input): the 256x capacity win transfers to a longer budget. 3 paired seeds (42/43/44): deltas -0.00413/-0.00394/-0.00476, mean -0.004276, paired-t -17.3, all-same-sign -- statistically indistinguishable from (slightly larger than) the frozen 2000-step win -0.00405. Baselines fell 0.9306@2000 -> ~0.910@2766 (compute effect), so cross-budget val_bpb is not comparable; only the within-budget paired delta is. The win is not a short-horizon artifact.","status":"exploratory","tags":["ngram","capacity","transfer","diagnostic","offbudget"]}
{"created_at":"2026-07-22T21:00:00-07:00","fingerprint":"8fd70d928a1fb847","followup_required":true,"observation_id":"evd_bug_fourgram_gate_uninitialized","possible_confounds":["distortion magnitude unquantified -- depends on the garbage values in memory"],"run_ids":["run_exp_ngram_fourgram_p_baseline_s42_903a6ad9d52977a4","run_exp_ngram_fourgram_p_fourgram_256_s42_8eddd00add2d9396","run_exp_ngram128_fourgra_cap256_s43_4d4c4e84c9b76fe4","run_exp_ngram128_fourgra_cap256_s44_a7efe8ae20e27424","run_exp_ngram128_fourgra_cap256_s45_94e265e8ce99d474","run_exp_ngram128_fourgra_cap128_fg256_s44_6ea0c3d0878c90ee","run_exp_ngram128_fourgra_cap128_fg256_s45_5156dde3f7ddef49","run_exp_ngram128_fourgra_cap128_fg256_s43_111e50f6b2ac4d40","run_exp_ngram128_fourgra_cap256_s46_a62bbb75a191d392","run_exp_ngram128_fourgra_cap128_fg256_s46_35483e207eeee8f2"],"statement":"fourgram_gate was never initialized in GPT.init_weights, so under meta-construction + model.to_empty(device) it held UNINITIALIZED GPU memory for every run with NGRAM_FOURGRAM_MULT>0 -- the 4-gram value embeddings were injected through a random, uncontrolled gate. init_weights explicitly zero-inits ve_gate, bigram_gate, trigram_gate and head_gate but omitted fourgram_gate. Found by the fable agent while implementing PK memory. Default runs unaffected (fourgram off -> gate is None). FIXED: guarded zeros_ init added; byte-identical for all default runs. The listed run_ids are the affected, now-untrustworthy runs.","status":"replicated","tags":["bug","fourgram","invalidates_results"]}
{"created_at": "2026-07-23T02:45:00-07:00", "fingerprint": "c28beed3526e6e96", "followup_required": true, "observation_id": "evd_obs_ngram_context_repeat_rate", "possible_confounds": ["Concatenates documents rather than doing best-fit row packing; this perturbs only the handful of positions at row boundaries.", "Counts occurrences over the whole stream, whereas a VE row's usable gradient signal also depends on WHEN in training the repeats occur.", "Singleton rate is measured on exact n-gram keys; the model sees HASHED keys, so collisions merge some singletons into shared rows, making true per-row starvation somewhat less severe than the exact numbers imply."], "run_ids": ["run_exp_fivegram_order_fivegram256_s42_cdf53a13b1964a4a"], "statement": "MEASURED n-gram context repeat statistics on the EXACT 294.9M-token training stream (lib tokenizer, shards in split order; the stream is exhausted inside shard 5, independently reconfirming the single-pass ~0.47-epoch regime). Exact mixed-radix keys, no hashing, so this is a property of the DATA, not of any particular table. bigram: 8.49M distinct, singleton 1.2%, median_occ 1468 | trigram: 70.4M distinct, singleton 15.9%, median_occ 26 | 4-gram: 164.8M distinct, singleton 45.8%, median_occ 2 | gappy4 (-4,-2,-1,0): 205.4M distinct, singleton 60.3%, median_occ 1 | 5-gram: 229.0M distinct, singleton 69.9%, median_occ 1. TWO CONSEQUENCES. (1) CONVERTS the data-starvation explanation for the 5-gram failure from interpretation into measurement: going 4->5-gram, the fraction of token positions whose context NEVER recurs jumps 45.8% -> 69.9%, and the median context is seen exactly once. A hashed VE row for a singleton context receives one gradient in the entire run and cannot generalize, so the 5-gram rung is starved BY CONSTRUCTION at this token budget -- quantitatively consistent with its measured -0.000470 against the 4-gram rung's -0.001206 on the same seeds 42-44. (2) REFUTED the design premise of the planned gappy-4gram probe. I had assumed equal TOKEN COUNT implies equal sparsity; it does not, because skipping a position decorrelates the tokens. gappy4 sits ~60% of the way from 4-gram to 5-gram in singleton rate, making it an INTERMEDIATE DOSE, not a matched control. The probe was re-specified as a dose-response test before any GPU time was spent.", "status": "exploratory", "tags": ["ngram", "sparsity", "data_budget", "observable", "mediator", "design_correction"]}
{"created_at": "2026-07-23T03:20:00-07:00", "fingerprint": "0fe688bf084f680c", "followup_required": false, "observation_id": "evd_obs_pipeline_not_bitwise_deterministic", "possible_confounds": ["Measured at step 29 (early, high-curvature training) where relative divergence is larger than at step 2000; the final-metric analogue is the documented sigma_repro=0.00031.", "The three runs were not pinned to one physical GPU, so a device-to-device kernel-selection difference is folded into the reported nondeterminism.", "Root cause attributed to max-autotune by plausibility, not isolated by rerunning with COMPILE_MODE=default."], "run_ids": ["run_exp_fivegram_order_sota004_s42_04a048e19d2fd122"], "statement": "The training pipeline is NOT bitwise reproducible run-to-run at fixed seed and fixed code. Measured directly with three 30-step runs of the SOTA #4 config, seed 42, same GPU class: train.py v10 run1 val_loss@29 = 5.068482; v10 run2 (IDENTICAL binary) = 5.066724; v11 with NGRAM_FOURGRAM_SPAN=3 = 5.070265. The v10-vs-ITSELF gap is 0.001758 and the v10-vs-v11 gap is 0.001783 -- the SAME magnitude. The code change is therefore statistically indistinguishable from the pipeline's own nondeterminism (most plausibly torch.compile max-autotune kernel selection plus non-deterministic reduction order; not further isolated). WHY THIS WAS CHECKED: the v11 span lever LOOKED provably inert at span=3 (static diff confirms far_idx is bound to the SAME OBJECT as prev3_idx, so the hash expression is literally unchanged, and peak VRAM matched to 0.5MB), but 'provably inert by inspection' is exactly the reasoning that produced the fourgram_gate false negative and cost 10 retracted runs. The v10-vs-v10 control was run specifically to avoid repeating that error, and it resolved the question. CONSEQUENCE 1: reusing the exp_fivegram_order sota004 arm as the control for exp_gappy_fourgram_dose is VALID -- the code-hash change does not bias it -- but those controls carry ordinary run-to-run noise. CONSEQUENCE 2 (WORDING CORRECTION): this campaign has repeatedly described new env-gated levers as 'byte-identical when off'. That claim was always verified via sparse-grad capture-site counts and peak VRAM (STRUCTURAL identity -- no added ops, no added memory), never via bitwise-equal loss, which the measurement above shows is not even attainable for a rerun of the same binary. The correct claim is 'structurally identical / no added ops or memory when off', and prior 'byte-identical' phrasing in reconciliation notes and commit messages should be read that way. This does NOT weaken any experimental result: every comparison in the campaign is paired-by-seed with an explicitly modelled noise floor (sigma_seed=0.000847, sigma_repro=0.00031 on final val_bpb), and run-to-run noise is already inside that floor.", "status": "replicated", "tags": ["integrity", "reproducibility", "determinism", "methodology", "wording_correction"]}
{"created_at": "2026-07-23T04:25:00-07:00", "fingerprint": "514b250716dcd4eb", "followup_required": false, "observation_id": "evd_obs_sota_snapshots_were_not_runnable", "possible_confounds": ["The omission affected standalone reproduction only; no experimental result depended on the snapshots, since all runs executed against the live working tree where observable.py is present."], "run_ids": ["run_exp_fourgram_holdout_fourgram256_s46_113b9734c052d588"], "statement": "ALL FOUR SOTA snapshots in research/sota_snapshots/ were NOT REPRODUCIBLE as shipped. Each contained train.py, lib.py, prepare.py, data_split.json and repro.json, but OMITTED observable.py, which train.py imports unconditionally ('from observable import OBS, build_step_observables, format_observable_line'). Running the sota_004 snapshot end-to-end on an H200 with the exact env recorded in its repro.json failed immediately: ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'observable'. HOW IT WAS FOUND: by actually EXECUTING a snapshot instead of trusting its file list and matching hashes. The four recorded code hashes all verified correctly -- hash verification confirms the files present are the right ones, but says nothing about files that were never included, so it could never have caught this. The reproducibility ritual had been performed four times and never once tested. FIX: observable.py added to all four snapshots, its sha256 recorded in each repro.json alongside the existing hashes, and a REPRODUCIBILITY_FIX note written into each. GENERAL LESSON: 'snapshot the code' must be validated by running the snapshot from a clean directory, not by enumerating files believed to matter. A dependency that is imported unconditionally is exactly the kind of thing a hand-maintained file list omits silently.", "status": "replicated", "tags": ["integrity", "reproducibility", "snapshot", "process_defect"]}
{"created_at": "2026-07-23T04:55:00-07:00", "fingerprint": "fa4d95e0ec0d5ae5", "followup_required": false, "observation_id": "evd_obs_sota004_snapshot_reproduces", "possible_confounds": ["Single seed (46). Reproduction of the 6-seed hold-out MEAN 0.922710 is inferred from this one seed matching, not directly re-measured.", "Run on the same machine and software stack as the original; this validates snapshot completeness and recorded values, NOT portability to a different environment."], "run_ids": ["run_exp_fourgram_holdout_fourgram256_s46_113b9734c052d588"], "statement": "END-TO-END REPRODUCTION of the sota_004 snapshot CONFIRMED, after the missing-observable.py fix. The snapshot was copied to a CLEAN remote directory (train.py/lib.py/prepare.py/data_split.json/observable.py only, sha256-verified on arrival) and run on an H200 with exactly the env recorded in its repro.json (STOP_MODE=steps MAX_STEPS=2000 ATTN_BACKEND=sdpa SEED=46 NGRAM_TABLE_MULT=256 NGRAM_FOURGRAM_MULT=256 DOC_MASK=1 DOC_MASK_MODE=seg WARMDOWN_RATIO=0.75 NGRAM_STATE_ROWWISE=1 NGRAM_SPARSE_GRAD=1). RESULT: val_bpb 0.923582 vs the recorded hold-out seed-46 value 0.923642 -- difference -0.000060, i.e. 0.19 sigma_repro (0.00031) and 0.07 sigma_seed (0.000847). Peak VRAM 76587.6MB, matching the recorded 76.6GB for SOTA #4 exactly, which also confirms the rowwise-state + sparse-grad infrastructure the configuration depends on is correctly captured. This is the FIRST time any SOTA snapshot in this campaign was actually executed. It validates both the snapshot contents and the recorded number, and it is the check that exposed [[evd_obs_sota_snapshots_were_not_runnable]]. Exact bitwise equality is neither expected nor achievable here -- see [[evd_obs_pipeline_not_bitwise_deterministic]]; agreement is asserted at the sigma_repro level, which is the correct standard for this pipeline.", "status": "replicated", "tags": ["integrity", "reproducibility", "snapshot", "validated"]}
